      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that the pressure (\\(P\\)) of a sample of gas is inversely proportional to its volume (\\(V\\)) at constant temperature, we can write the relationship as:\n\n\\[ P \\propto \\frac{1}{V} \\]\n\nThis can be expressed mathematically as:\n\n\\[ P_1 V_1 = P_2 V_2 \\]\n\nwhere \\(P_1\\) and \\(V_1\\) are the initial pressure and volume, and \\(P_2\\) and \\(V_2\\) are the final pressure and volume.\n\nWe are given:\n- \\(P_1 = 5 \\text{ kPa}\\)\n- \\(V_1 = 2.28 \\text{ liters}\\)\n- \\(V_2 = 5.7 \\text{ liters}\\)\n\nWe need to find \\(P_2\\). Using the relationship \\(P_1 V_1 = P_2 V_2\\), we can substitute the given values:\n\n\\[ 5 \\text{ kPa} \\times 2.28 \\text{ liters} = P_2 \\times 5.7 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nSolving for \\(P_2\\):\n\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{5 \\text{ kPa} \\times 2.28 \\text{ liters}}{5.7 \\text{ liters}} \\]\n\nFirst, perform the multiplication on the left side:\n\n\\[ 5 \\times 2.28 = 11.4 \\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4 \\text{ kPa} \\cdot \\text{liters}}{5.7 \\text{ liters}} \\]\n\nNow, divide the numerator by the denominator:\n\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4}{5.7} = 2 \\text{ kPa} \\]\n\nTherefore, the new pressure \\(P_2\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) kPa.",
